This fixture is DISTRIBUTION-ONLY. It must never be copied to a consumer.

Its subject is scripts/validate-enforcement-map.sh — the same subject as its sibling
enforcement-map-sites, and not shipped to consumers for the same reason: the validator
checks the distribution's own two writers against each other, and a consumer has neither.
A fixture whose subject is absent cannot run. It is permanently dormant, and a dormant
fixture in the consumer's suite is worse than no fixture, because the catalog then claims
the check is self-tested when nothing tests it there.

WHY IT IS A SECOND FIXTURE AND NOT MORE ASSERTIONS ON THE FIRST.

enforcement-map-sites is the suite's single largest unit and therefore its wall-clock
floor: it invokes the validator 42 times against 25 mutated trees, and no amount of
parallelism in the runner beats one fixture that takes longer than the pool. Folding these
eight assertions into it would have added their cost directly to that floor. Here they run
concurrently with it and add no wall clock at all.

The split is also the direction the suite is going. The two fixtures have different
subjects in the same file: the sibling asserts that the SITE TABLE and the packaging joins
cannot go quiet; this one asserts that the invariants which evaluate a predicate once per
item — per manifest row, per map entry, per fixture, per role, per rule-prose file, per
seed — still reach every item. Those are different questions and they fail differently.

THE MARKER IS THE SOURCE OF TRUTH. install.sh, preclassify.sh's map_consumer() and I8 all
derive the dist-only set from this file's presence rather than from a list any of them has
to remember to update — see the sibling's marker for the release where a hand-maintained
list put a subject-less fixture on the reference consumer.
